  <h1>Common Pregnancy Discrimination Misconceptions</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Riley Chen (RC), Employment &amp; Civil Rights Practice Lead. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>&ldquo;My employer can ask whether I plan to get pregnant.&rdquo;</h2><p>No. Questions about family planning are discriminatory under Title VII and the PDA. Employers cannot use anticipated pregnancy as a factor in hiring, promotion, or other employment decisions.</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;The PWFA only applies to large employers.&rdquo;</h2><p>The PWFA covers employers with 15+ employees — the same threshold as Title VII. However, many states have their own pregnancy accommodation laws that cover smaller employers or provide broader protections.</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;I can only claim discrimination if I was fired.&rdquo;</h2><p>No. Failure to accommodate, demotion, pay reduction, denial of promotion, hostile environment, and forced unpaid leave are all actionable. You don&rsquo;t need to be terminated to have a viable pregnancy discrimination claim.</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;I can&rsquo;t be discriminated against while on FMLA.&rdquo;</h2><p>FMLA provides job protection during leave, but discrimination based on pregnancy is separately prohibited under the PDA regardless of FMLA status. Retaliation for taking FMLA leave is a separate claim. Both may apply to the same adverse action.</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;The employer can require a doctor&rsquo;s note for every pregnancy accommodation.&rdquo;</h2><p>Under the PWFA, employers may request documentation for accommodation requests, but only when the limitation or need is not obvious. Demanding extensive documentation for obviously pregnancy-related restrictions may itself violate the PWFA.</p>
  <h2>Where to go from here</h2>
  <p>See <a href="/guides/how-pregnancy-discrimination-damages-work">how damages work</a> and <a href="/guides/types-of-pregnancy-discrimination">types of discrimination</a>.</p>
